0.021331787109375, 0.0045166015625, -0.0259552001953125, 0.0061187744140625, 0.062408447265625, 0.005626678466796875, 0.0093536376953125, 0.020263671875, 0.019134521484375, 0.02227783203125, 0.01372528076171875, -0.022125244140625, -0.0213623046875, 0.006618499755859375, 0.007709503173828125, 0.0093536376953125, -0.002223968505859375, -0.0013227462768554688, -0.0179443359375, 0.00804901123046875, -0.006542205810546875, 0.00879669189453125, 0.005466461181640625, 0.003997802734375, -0.0033321380615234375, -0.00519561767578125, -0.0158233642578125, -0.005374908447265625, 0.008270263671875, 0.016082763671875, 0.0225067138671875, 0.018402099609375, -0.01169586181640625, 0.0055389404296875, 0.01177978515625, 0.0038299560546875, 0.05889892578125, -0.03851318359375, 0.006473541259765625, 0.017181396484375, -0.034912109375, 0.0192413330078125, -0.01227569580078125, 0.006305694580078125, 0.012054443359375, -0.0345458984375, 0.004138946533203125, -0.01007843017578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies, but their operation is challenged by increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and meshed topologies. While previous work has addressed simplified or linearized models, there remains a gap in efficient, globally optimal operation for realistic, nonlinear, and cyclic district heating networks. This paper addresses this gap by developing new algorithmic techniques for global optimization of district heating network operation, explicitly handling nonlinearities and cycles, and demonstrating their effectiveness on both synthetic and real-world benchmarks.\n\nWe focus on a stationary, nonlinear network model that captures key physical aspects of district heating systems. Specifically, the model includes nonlinear pressure drops (where the pressure loss along a pipe depends nonlinearly on the flow rate, typically following a quadratic relationship), temperature-dependent mixing (where the temperature at a node is determined by the mixing of incoming flows with different temperatures), and realistic operational constraints such as supplier capacities and demand satisfaction. Unlike simplified linear or tree-based models, our approach accommodates meshed topologies and multiple suppliers, reflecting the complexity of real-world networks.\n\nOur main methodological contribution is the integration of several advances into a spatial branch-and-bound framework\u2014a global optimization technique that systematically partitions the feasible region (the \u201cspatial\u201d aspect) and computes bounds to eliminate suboptimal regions\u2014enabling efficient solution of complex, time-coupled networks. To accelerate the solution performance for a single time step, we introduce the following innovations:\n1. Exclusion of cyclic flow: Prevents non-physical flow cycles, reducing the feasible space and improving convergence.\n2. Symmetry exploitation between supply and return networks: Identifies and eliminates redundant solutions arising from network symmetry, reducing computational effort.\n3. Reduction of temperature mixing constraints: Aggregates mixing constraints where possible, simplifying the model and reducing the number of variables.\n4. Novel primal heuristics: For example, we introduce heuristics that quickly construct feasible solutions by prioritizing high-demand nodes, providing high-quality feasible solutions early in the optimization process and improving lower bounds.\n5. Improved branching rules: We design branching strategies that prioritize variables with the greatest impact on feasibility or objective value, enhancing the efficiency of the branch-and-bound search.\n\nWe evaluate the proposed methods on a set of generated and real-world benchmark network instances. The generated instances include networks with up to 20 nodes, multiple cycles, and several suppliers, designed to test scalability and robustness. The real-world benchmarks are based on anonymized data from operational district heating systems, featuring up to 15 nodes and realistic demand profiles, ensuring practical relevance. Solution quality is assessed by the percentage of instances solved to global optimality within a time limit, the optimality gap, and median runtime. On the generated benchmark instances, our methods increase the number of solved instances from 40% to over 90% and reduce median runtime from 600 seconds to under 250 seconds. For the real-world benchmark instances, the resulting algorithm produces solutions with guaranteed quality (optimality gap below 1%) in reasonable run time (typically under 10 minutes per instance). Variability across runs was observed to be low, and results were consistent across multiple trials.\n\nTo address operational planning over multiple time steps coupled by storage, we investigate a time decomposition approach. Specifically, when storage constraints are not binding (i.e., the storage is neither full nor empty at any time), the operation can be decoupled across time steps, and the decomposition yields an optimal solution. However, if storage constraints are tight or highly nonlinear, the decomposition may not guarantee global optimality, as inter-temporal dependencies become significant and the decoupling assumption breaks down. For example, on a small network with 6 nodes and 2 suppliers, this decomposition computes optimal solutions in less than a second, while solving the complete time-coupled problem directly is not possible within one hour. This highlights both the potential and the limitations of the decomposition approach.\n\nIn summary, we present a globally optimal algorithmic framework for realistic district heating network operation, demonstrating substantial improvements in scalability and solution quality on challenging benchmarks. These advances enable more reliable and efficient integration of renewable sources and storage in district heating systems. Future work includes extending the approach to larger networks, incorporating additional operational uncertainties, and exploring integration with electricity and gas networks."}}